---
// Security v2 (2026-07-03) — "Plain Words, Same Teeth" pass: this page
// is the plain-terms overview (each posture pillar leads with a plain
// sentence; the precise, falsifiable fact rides as mono small print),
// canonically deferring to /trust/security-overview for parameter-level
// depth. Fleet v2 recipes (PageHero/Section/IconTile/CtaBand, AA-safe
// accent text). Every pinned security claim — TLS/HSTS, scrypt logN=15,
// webhook HMAC shape, threat model, retention, egress — is byte-identical
// to the pre-redesign page (W306/W313/W321/W325/W332/W333 baselines).
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import PageHero from '../components/PageHero.astro';
import Section from '../components/Section.astro';
import IconTile from '../components/IconTile.astro';
import CtaBand from '../components/CtaBand.astro';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Security"
  description="How Driftstack protects customer keys and content. TLS 1.2+1.3 with HSTS preload, scrypt-hashed API keys, HMAC-SHA256 webhook signatures, no-customer-data-access posture, EU-resident infrastructure."
  ogImage="/og/security.png"
>
  <!-- Hero -->
  <PageHero label="Security" title="We don't see your traffic. We can't read your keys.">
    <p class="mt-5 max-w-2xl text-lg leading-8 text-tk-ink-2">
      Driftstack's security posture starts from a single principle:
      Driftstack should never have a copy of anything that would let us
      impersonate you, intercept your traffic, or replay your sessions.
      The architecture below enforces that — not as policy, as code.
    </p>
    <p class="mt-4 max-w-2xl text-sm text-tk-ink-3">
      This page is security in plain terms. The deep technical posture —
      parameter by parameter, control by control — lives at
      <a href="/trust/security-overview" class="text-tk-accent-text underline"
        >/trust/security-overview</a
      >.
    </p>
  </PageHero>

  <!-- Six-pillar layout — each pillar: plain-language line first, the
       precise claim as mono small print, then the full paragraph. -->
  <Section label="The posture" title="Six promises, built into the architecture.">
    <div class="mt-12 grid gap-12 md:grid-cols-2">
      <!-- Pillar 1 -->
      <div>
        <IconTile class="mb-3">
          <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="16" height="16" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.75" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true">
            <path d="M2 12h20M12 2v20" /><circle cx="12" cy="12" r="9" />
          </svg>
        </IconTile>
        <p class="font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-widest text-tk-accent-text">
          01 · Transport
        </p>
        <h3 class="mt-3 text-2xl font-semibold text-tk-ink">
          Everything between you and us travels encrypted.
        </h3>
        <p class="mt-2 font-mono text-xs text-tk-ink-3">TLS 1.2 + 1.3, HSTS preload-eligible.</p>
        <p class="mt-3 text-tk-ink-2">
          All inbound traffic is HTTPS — Cloudflare terminates TLS
          at the edge with full (strict) origin validation against
          our Hetzner host. The API server speaks TLS 1.2 / 1.3
          only and sets a 2-year HSTS header with
          <code>includeSubDomains</code> + <code>preload</code>.
          No plaintext HTTP on any customer-facing path.
          Authentication is Bearer API key, not client-certificate.
        </p>
      </div>

      <!-- Pillar 2 -->
      <div>
        <IconTile class="mb-3">
          <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="16" height="16" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.75" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true">
            <path d="M5 12h14M12 5l7 7-7 7" />
          </svg>
        </IconTile>
        <p class="font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-widest text-tk-accent-text">
          02 · Egress
        </p>
        <h3 class="mt-3 text-2xl font-semibold text-tk-ink">
          Sessions leave through the connection you choose.
        </h3>
        <p class="mt-2 font-mono text-xs text-tk-ink-3">Customer-configurable egress, per profile.</p>
        <p class="mt-3 text-tk-ink-2">
          Each profile can attach its own egress configuration —
          SOCKS5 (with full UDP / WebRTC / QUIC tunnelling), OpenVPN
          (.ovpn file), or WireGuard (.conf). Sessions bound to a
          profile dial the tunnel before launching the browser; DNS
          leaks blocked. Without an attached config, session traffic
          exits via Driftstack-managed infrastructure. We never
          store destination response bodies. See
          <a href="/trust/security-overview" class="text-tk-accent-text underline">/trust/security-overview</a>
          for the full posture.
        </p>
      </div>

      <!-- Pillar 3 -->
      <div>
        <IconTile class="mb-3">
          <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="16" height="16" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.75" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true">
            <circle cx="9" cy="12" r="7" /><path d="M16 12h6M19 9v6" />
          </svg>
        </IconTile>
        <p class="font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-widest text-tk-accent-text">
          03 · API keys
        </p>
        <h3 class="mt-3 text-2xl font-semibold text-tk-ink">
          We couldn't read your keys if we tried.
        </h3>
        <p class="mt-2 font-mono text-xs text-tk-ink-3">scrypt at rest, never in plaintext.</p>
        <p class="mt-3 text-tk-ink-2">
          API keys are hashed with scrypt (logN=15) before they touch
          the database. Plaintext is returned exactly once, on creation;
          after that there is no path — admin, support, ops — to recover
          it. A database breach surfaces hashes, not keys. Auth runtime
          uses a 30-second sha256-keyed cache so verification stays
          fast without weakening at-rest strength.
        </p>
      </div>

      <!-- Pillar 4 -->
      <div>
        <IconTile class="mb-3">
          <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="16" height="16" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.75" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true">
            <path d="M22 12h-4l-3 9L9 3l-3 9H2" />
          </svg>
        </IconTile>
        <p class="font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-widest text-tk-accent-text">
          04 · Webhooks
        </p>
        <h3 class="mt-3 text-2xl font-semibold text-tk-ink">
          Every webhook proves it came from us.
        </h3>
        <p class="mt-2 font-mono text-xs text-tk-ink-3">HMAC-SHA256, replay-windowed.</p>
        <p class="mt-3 text-tk-ink-2">
          Outbound webhook deliveries carry an X-Driftstack-Signature header
          in the form <code class="font-mono">t=&lt;timestamp&gt;,v1=&lt;hex&gt;</code>.
          Customers verify with the SDK's
          <code class="font-mono">verifyWebhookSignature</code> helper
          (constant-time compare). The default 5-minute timestamp
          tolerance protects against replay; secrets rotate per-endpoint
          without touching the rest of your account.
        </p>
      </div>

      <!-- Pillar 5 -->
      <div>
        <IconTile class="mb-3">
          <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="16" height="16" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.75" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true">
            <circle cx="12" cy="7" r="4" /><path d="M5 21v-2a4 4 0 0 1 4-4h6a4 4 0 0 1 4 4v2" /><path d="M9 7l2 2 4-4" />
          </svg>
        </IconTile>
        <p class="font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-widest text-tk-accent-text">
          05 · Team RBAC
        </p>
        <h3 class="mt-3 text-2xl font-semibold text-tk-ink">
          Your whole team can look. Only admins can change.
        </h3>
        <p class="mt-2 font-mono text-xs text-tk-ink-3">Admin / member roles, scope-gated writes.</p>
        <p class="mt-3 text-tk-ink-2">
          Team members read the owner's resources but only admin-role
          members can write. Sessions, profiles, webhooks, API keys,
          email preferences — every write route enforces the role
          check at the route layer + audit-logs the actor (member id)
          separately from the resource owner. Team owners review who
          did what on their account at any time via
          <code class="font-mono">/v1/account/audit-log</code>.
        </p>
      </div>

      <!-- Pillar 6 — full width -->
      <div class="md:col-span-2">
        <IconTile class="mb-3">
          <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="16" height="16" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.75" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true">
            <path d="M12 2L4 5v6c0 5.5 3.8 10.7 8 11.8 4.2-1.1 8-6.3 8-11.8V5l-8-3z" /><path d="M9 12l2 2 4-4" />
          </svg>
        </IconTile>
        <p class="font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-widest text-tk-accent-text">
          06 · No-customer-data-access posture
        </p>
        <h3 class="mt-3 text-2xl font-semibold text-tk-ink">
          Nobody at Driftstack can watch your sessions.
        </h3>
        <p class="mt-2 font-mono text-xs text-tk-ink-3">
          Driftstack staff cannot read your sessions — metadata only, by design.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-3 max-w-prose text-tk-ink-2">
          Driftstack's control plane stores license metadata, session
          metadata (id, lifecycle status, timestamps), and aggregate
          usage counters. It does not store the session content itself.
          URLs visited, form data submitted, screenshots captured, DOM
          snapshots, browser cookies — these never reach our infra. For
          self-hosted deployments, even the metadata stays inside your
          network; only license-validity heartbeats reach our servers.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </Section>

  <!-- What we don't claim -->
  <Section label="What we don't claim" title="Honest scope." class="bg-tk-surface">
    <ul class="mt-8 grid gap-4 md:grid-cols-2">
      <li class="card p-6 text-sm text-tk-ink-2">
        <IconTile class="mb-3">
          <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="14" height="14" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true">
            <circle cx="12" cy="12" r="10" />
            <path d="M4.93 4.93l14.14 14.14" />
          </svg>
        </IconTile>
        <strong class="block text-tk-ink">No SOC 2.</strong>
        SOC 2 is not yet certified. We'll start the audit when the
        audit cost matches the customer profile that asks for it.
        We say so plainly rather than implying compliance via vague
        "enterprise-grade" language.
      </li>
      <li class="card p-6 text-sm text-tk-ink-2">
        <IconTile class="mb-3">
          <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="14" height="14" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true">
            <circle cx="12" cy="12" r="10" />
            <path d="M4.93 4.93l14.14 14.14" />
          </svg>
        </IconTile>
        <strong class="block text-tk-ink">No ISO 27001.</strong>
        Same answer. The framework's posture matters; the certificate
        itself follows when the audit fits the budget.
      </li>
      <li class="card p-6 text-sm text-tk-ink-2">
        <IconTile class="mb-3">
          <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="14" height="14" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true">
            <rect x="3" y="4" width="18" height="16" rx="2" />
            <path d="M3 9h18M9 4v16" />
          </svg>
        </IconTile>
        <strong class="block text-tk-ink">Sub-processors are listed.</strong>
        Hetzner, Neon, Upstash, Cloudflare, Postmark, Sentry, Stripe,
        Anthropic, Moneybird, MacStadium. Full list with purpose +
        jurisdiction at <a href="/trust/sub-processors" class="text-tk-accent-text underline">/trust/sub-processors</a>.
      </li>
      <li class="card p-6 text-sm text-tk-ink-2">
        <IconTile class="mb-3">
          <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="14" height="14" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true">
            <path d="M12 22s-8-4.5-8-11.8A8 8 0 0 1 12 2a8 8 0 0 1 8 8.2c0 7.3-8 11.8-8 11.8z" />
            <circle cx="12" cy="10" r="3" />
          </svg>
        </IconTile>
        <strong class="block text-tk-ink">Data residency is EU-default.</strong>
        Compute, database, object storage all in the EU (Hetzner FSN
        + Neon EU + Upstash EU + Cloudflare R2 EU). Session execution
        may run elsewhere under SCCs + EU-US DPF. Detail in the same
        sub-processors list.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </Section>

  <!-- V-503 — defense in depth -->
  <Section
    label="Defense in depth"
    title="Layered controls. No single line of defense."
    lead="Each layer below is independently effective; an attacker would have to bypass every one to reach customer keys or session content. We assume any single layer can fail and design the next layer to survive it."
  >
    <div class="mt-10 grid gap-6 md:grid-cols-2">
      <div class="card p-6">
        <IconTile class="mb-3">
          <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="14" height="14" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.75" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true">
            <path d="M2 12s3-7 10-7 10 7 10 7-3 7-10 7-10-7-10-7z" /><circle cx="12" cy="12" r="3" />
          </svg>
        </IconTile>
        <p class="font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-widest text-tk-accent-text">
          Edge
        </p>
        <h3 class="mt-2 text-lg font-medium text-tk-ink">
          Cloudflare TLS 1.3 + WAF
        </h3>
        <p class="mt-3 text-sm text-tk-ink-2">
          All inbound traffic terminates at Cloudflare's edge. Full
          (strict) SSL/TLS validation against Let's Encrypt origin
          certificates ensures the request actually reached our origin
          and not a man-in-the-middle. Bot Fight Mode + WAF rules block
          automated abuse before it reaches the origin.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="card p-6">
        <IconTile class="mb-3">
          <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="14" height="14" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.75" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true">
            <rect x="3" y="6" width="18" height="12" rx="2" /><path d="M3 10h18" />
          </svg>
        </IconTile>
        <p class="font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-widest text-tk-accent-text">
          Origin
        </p>
        <h3 class="mt-2 text-lg font-medium text-tk-ink">
          nginx hardening + UFW + fail2ban
        </h3>
        <p class="mt-3 text-sm text-tk-ink-2">
          Hetzner origin host runs nginx with TLS 1.2 + 1.3 only,
          Mozilla intermediate cipher suite, HSTS preload-eligible. UFW
          firewall locks the host to ports 22 (SSH), 80 (redirect to
          443), 443 (TLS). fail2ban bans repeat-offender IPs at the
          kernel level.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="card p-6">
        <IconTile class="mb-3">
          <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="14" height="14" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.75" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true">
            <rect x="2" y="3" width="20" height="14" rx="2" /><path d="M8 21h8M12 17v4" />
          </svg>
        </IconTile>
        <p class="font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-widest text-tk-accent-text">
          Application
        </p>
        <h3 class="mt-2 text-lg font-medium text-tk-ink">
          Auth gate + scope check + rate limit
        </h3>
        <p class="mt-3 text-sm text-tk-ink-2">
          Every authenticated route runs through three middleware
          layers: auth (validates the API key + loads account context),
          scope check (enforces granular per-resource permissions like
          <code class="font-mono">read:sessions</code>), rate limit (per-account token
          buckets backed by Redis with Postgres fallback). Failures
          short-circuit the request before it touches a service.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="card p-6">
        <IconTile class="mb-3">
          <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="14" height="14" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.75" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true">
            <ellipse cx="12" cy="5" rx="9" ry="3" /><path d="M3 5v6c0 1.7 4 3 9 3s9-1.3 9-3V5" /><path d="M3 11v6c0 1.7 4 3 9 3s9-1.3 9-3v-6" />
          </svg>
        </IconTile>
        <p class="font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-widest text-tk-accent-text">
          Data
        </p>
        <h3 class="mt-2 text-lg font-medium text-tk-ink">
          Encryption at rest + isolation
        </h3>
        <p class="mt-3 text-sm text-tk-ink-2">
          API keys are scrypt-hashed with per-key salts; recovery is
          impossible by design. Account passwords use the same KDF.
          MFA TOTP secrets, BYOK Anthropic keys, and gui_control_keys
          all live in AES-256-GCM ciphertext under the same
          host-resident encryption key, decrypted in-memory only at
          execution time and never logged. Postgres + Redis carriers
          provide encryption at rest by default at the storage layer.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="card p-6">
        <IconTile class="mb-3">
          <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="14" height="14" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.75" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true">
            <path d="M14 2H6a2 2 0 0 0-2 2v16a2 2 0 0 0 2 2h12a2 2 0 0 0 2-2V8z" /><path d="M14 2v6h6" /><path d="M9 13h6M9 17h4" />
          </svg>
        </IconTile>
        <p class="font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-widest text-tk-accent-text">
          Audit
        </p>
        <h3 class="mt-2 text-lg font-medium text-tk-ink">
          Append-only customer audit log
        </h3>
        <p class="mt-3 text-sm text-tk-ink-2">
          Every customer-visible event lands in an append-only audit
          log: API key lifecycle, MFA enrollment, profile changes,
          webhook secret rotations, login + password changes. Customers
          export the full log via the dashboard or
          <code class="font-mono">/v1/account/audit-log/export</code>
          (GDPR Article 20).
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="card p-6">
        <IconTile class="mb-3">
          <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="14" height="14" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.75" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true">
            <circle cx="11" cy="11" r="8" /><path d="M21 21l-4.35-4.35" />
          </svg>
        </IconTile>
        <p class="font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-widest text-tk-accent-text">
          Observability
        </p>
        <h3 class="mt-2 text-lg font-medium text-tk-ink">
          Sentry + structured logs
        </h3>
        <p class="mt-3 text-sm text-tk-ink-2">
          Server errors land in Sentry (EU region) with PII scrubbed.
          Request logs use Pino structured JSON; slow-query log fires
          on Postgres queries past a configurable threshold. No customer
          request body or response body is logged — only metadata
          sufficient to reconstruct what happened.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </Section>

  <!-- V-503 — threat model summary. Bespoke section shell (not the
       Section component): the W325.B baseline pins the rendered
       `>Threat model<` label form in this file's source. -->
  <section class="relative border-t border-tk-border bg-tk-surface">
    <div class="mx-auto max-w-6xl px-6 py-20 md:py-24">
      <p class="section-label">Threat model</p>
      <h2 class="mt-4 text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-tk-ink sm:text-3xl md:text-4xl">
        What we defend against. What we don't.
      </h2>
      <div class="mt-8 grid gap-12 md:grid-cols-2">
        <div>
          <h3 class="text-lg font-medium text-tk-ink">In scope</h3>
          <ul class="mt-4 space-y-3 text-sm text-tk-ink-2">
            <li class="flex gap-3">
              <span class="text-tk-accent-text">•</span>
              <span>
                <strong>API key compromise</strong> via leaked secret. Mitigation:
                scrypt-hashed at rest; rotation flow with 24-hour grace;
                granular per-resource scopes limit blast radius.
              </span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex gap-3">
              <span class="text-tk-accent-text">•</span>
              <span>
                <strong>Webhook signature forgery</strong>. Mitigation:
                HMAC-SHA256 signed deliveries with 5-minute timestamp
                tolerance; rotation flow dual-signs during grace.
              </span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex gap-3">
              <span class="text-tk-accent-text">•</span>
              <span>
                <strong>Session hijacking</strong> via cross-site or
                cross-account access. Mitigation: every route account-
                scopes resources at the database layer; cross-account
                lookups return 404, never 403.
              </span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex gap-3">
              <span class="text-tk-accent-text">•</span>
              <span>
                <strong>Brute-force auth attempts</strong>. Mitigation:
                per-IP rate limits on auth endpoints; fail2ban at the
                origin host level.
              </span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex gap-3">
              <span class="text-tk-accent-text">•</span>
              <span>
                <strong>Stolen browser session token</strong> (dashboard).
                Mitigation: MFA on sensitive ops; active-sign-ins page
                lets customers revoke; session tokens have configurable
                idle timeouts.
              </span>
            </li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h3 class="text-lg font-medium text-tk-ink">Explicitly out of scope</h3>
          <ul class="mt-4 space-y-3 text-sm text-tk-ink-2">
            <li class="flex gap-3">
              <span class="text-tk-accent-text">→</span>
              <span>
                <strong>Customer's destination response content.</strong>
                The control plane stores session metadata only — never
                response bodies. Whatever your code does inside a
                session is your threat model, not ours.
              </span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex gap-3">
              <span class="text-tk-accent-text">→</span>
              <span>
                <strong>Detection-vendor cat-and-mouse evolution.</strong>
                Our anti-detection posture is engine-level (Apple's
                WebKit source, not a runtime JS shim) and continuously
                updated; we can't promise forever-perfect parity
                against every detection vendor. See the
                <a href="/trust/cumulative-rig" class="text-tk-accent-text underline">cumulative-rig
                methodology</a> for what we do guarantee.
              </span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex gap-3">
              <span class="text-tk-accent-text">→</span>
              <span>
                <strong>Customer's keyboard / network at the endpoint.</strong>
                We can't protect a leaked API key on a customer
                developer's laptop or a webhook secret pasted into a
                misconfigured CI runner.
              </span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex gap-3">
              <span class="text-tk-accent-text">→</span>
              <span>
                <strong>Nation-state actors with sub-processor access.</strong>
                We assume the EU sub-processors (Hetzner, Neon, Upstash,
                Cloudflare) operate under their published security
                posture; we don't add additional anti-nation-state
                defense layers at the application tier.
              </span>
            </li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- V-503 — supply chain -->
  <Section label="Supply chain" title="Locked stack. Pinned versions. Audited dependencies.">
    <div class="mt-8 grid gap-8 md:grid-cols-3">
      <div>
        <h3 class="text-base font-medium text-tk-ink">Locked stack</h3>
        <p class="mt-3 text-sm text-tk-ink-2">
          Node 22 LTS, TypeScript strict, Fastify, Drizzle, Postgres 17,
          Redis 7. The stack rarely changes; every dependency-bump PR
          goes through `npm audit` review and a green test gate before
          it lands.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h3 class="text-base font-medium text-tk-ink">Dependabot + Renovate</h3>
        <p class="mt-3 text-sm text-tk-ink-2">
          Automated PRs for security advisories on direct + transitive
          deps. Auto-merge gated on passing CI; high-severity advisories
          page on-call within 24 hours of disclosure.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div>
